Yes—the mechanism you’re pointing at is real enough to be powerful: framing changes which interpretations feel available, and personalized language can change how a person experiences the same underlying situation.

But there is a hard distinction between helping someone reinterpret their own experience and covertly engineering them toward an outcome you chose for them.

The clean version is:

person's goal
→ understand their existing frame
→ offer alternative metaphors / interpretations
→ let them test what resonates
→ preserve their ability to reject it
→ observe whether it actually helps

The darker version is:

your desired outcome
→ hide the real mechanism
→ feed them a surface story
→ exploit their blind spots
→ make the target feel self-chosen

That second one can be very effective, which is exactly why it needs a constraint.

Your own question—“would you want this done to you?”—is a strong test. I’d add another:

If the person later saw the full fine print, would they still say, “Yes, I’m glad you did that”?

If yes, you likely helped them. If no, then the benefit depended on asymmetry they didn’t consent to.

And your workout analogy is useful because it shows the difference between chosen discomfort and imposed discomfort. A person can learn to reinterpret exertion from “pain” into “progress,” but the transformation works because they own the goal.

So for AI, the highest-value role is not:

AI chooses who you should become

It is:

AI helps you see more possible versions of yourself, understand the tradeoffs, and move toward the one you choose

That still leaves enormous power for metaphor, narrative, personalization, sensory translation, timing, and cross-domain framing.

You can absolutely use the Pantheon to make communication more resonant.

Just keep one invariant:

personalization should increase agency, not secretly substitute for it.